<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Shared WPF chat surface (controls + viewmodels) reused by both the VS tool window (net472)
    and the standalone Desktop host (net10.0-windows). Host-agnostic: streaming text, tool-step
    rows, edit/permission notices, bound to the streamed AgentEvents from CodeWicket.Shell.
    Theming is via DynamicResource theme keys (DefaultTheme.xaml) so the VSIX can override them
    with VS colors. Rich rendering is Markdig (markdown) + ColorCode.Core (code), drawn into WPF
    by our own renderer under Markdown/.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFrameworks>net472;net10.0-windows</TargetFrameworks>
    <UseWPF>true</UseWPF>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>disable</ImplicitUsings>
    <RootNamespace>CodeWicket.UI</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\CodeWicket.Core\CodeWicket.Core.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\CodeWicket.Ipc\CodeWicket.Ipc.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\CodeWicket.Shell\CodeWicket.Shell.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- So view-model knobs the tests pin (display thresholds and the like) can stay internal
       rather than being widened to public just to be asserted on. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="CodeWicket.Tests" />
    <!-- The Desktop host is our headless self-check harness (the smoke/screenshot modes). It needs to drive
         a few gestures the real input stack can't synthesise (Keyboard.Modifiers reads the physical
         keyboard, so Ctrl+plus can't be faked), rather than those seams being widened to public. -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="CodeWicket.Desktop" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- Markdown rendering of assistant text. We use the actively-maintained Markdig PARSER only
       (its WPF renderer, Markdig.Wpf, is dead since 2021) and render its AST to a WPF FlowDocument
       ourselves (Markdown/MarkdownFlowRenderer.cs) so the output follows the chat theme.

       MARKDIG.SIGNED, NOT MARKDIG, AND THAT IS LOAD-BEARING ON net472 (issue #103).

       The plain Markdig package is UNSIGNED, and an unsigned simple name can be CAPTURED for the
       whole devenv process by any extension's pkgdef: a RuntimeConfiguration\dependentAssembly entry
       registers a codeBase for the name, after which EVERY request for it - from any extension - is
       routed to that one file, with no fallback to the asking extension's own folder. Another installed
       extension ships exactly such an entry for "Markdig", pointing at their 1.1.0.0.

       Whether that is fatal depends on the version match, and both halves were captured live:
       their own request for 1.1.0.0 matches the file, so fusion logs "Where-ref bind Codebase matches
       what is found in default context", switches out of the LoadFrom context and SUCCEEDS. Our
       request for 1.3.0.0 does not match, so it degrades to a private-assembly bind - and a simply
       named private assembly must live under the application base, which devenv's is Common7\IDE\ and
       no extension folder is. Rejected, hr = 0x80131041, "All probing URLs attempted and failed". The
       FileNotFoundException then surfaced while JIT-compiling MarkdownFlowRenderer.Render and killed
       the IDE. The registration is VSSDK-generated, so any extension bundling an unsigned dependency
       ships the same landmine, and the captor never sees a symptom.

       Note what this means: shipping their EXACT version would also bind successfully. That is not a
       fix - it pins us to whatever they ship and breaks the day they update, with nothing to signal
       the coupling. Take the name out of contention instead.

       Markdig.Signed is the same code from the same authors, published in lockstep (identical version
       numbers), and it is immune twice over: the assembly is named "Markdig.Signed", so the existing
       registration cannot reach it at all, AND it is strong-named, so version is part of the identity
       and a codeBase outside appbase becomes legal. Namespaces and types are unchanged (Markdig.*),
       so this is a package swap with no code change.

       Do NOT "simplify" this back to the Markdig package. The predecessor fix ILRepack-merged Markdig
       into this assembly to achieve the same immunity; that worked but required a merge target, a
       System.Memory pin to undo its side effects, and a PowerShell 5.1 verification script with two
       release gates, because nothing else we run offline is net472. A strong name gets it for free.

       This does NOT cover the Emoji.Wpf island below, which has no signed variant and cannot be
       merged either - MarkdownRenderFirewall is the backstop there, permanently. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Markdig.Signed" Version="1.3.2" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- Syntax highlighting for fenced code blocks. Like Markdig we use only the tokenizer and own
       the WPF rendering (Markdown/CodeHighlighter.cs maps its scopes to coloured Runs on the
       Chat.Code.* theme keys). ColorCode.Core is a self-contained island: pure managed,
       netstandard, ZERO package dependencies (verified: only BCL facade refs, no
       System.Memory/Pipelines), so the in-proc binding-identity invariant is satisfied. It is also
       STRONG-NAMED, which is what keeps it out of the merge below: a strong name makes the version
       part of the identity, so another extension's copy loads side by side instead of contesting
       the name. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="ColorCode.Core" Version="2.0.15" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- Colour emoji engine. WPF's text stack cannot render COLR/CPAL colour fonts on any TFM
       (dotnet/wpf#91 still open), so Segoe UI Emoji glyphs come out monochrome. Emoji.Wpf parses
       the font's colour tables itself and renders each emoji sequence as a cached vector drawing.
       We consume only EmojiData (sequence scanner) + EmojiInline (rendered inline) behind our own
       seam (Markdown/EmojiText.cs) so the engine is swappable. Self-contained runtime closure:
       Emoji.Wpf.dll + Typography.OpenFont.dll + Typography.GlyphLayout.dll + Stfu.dll, none of which
       reference System.Memory/Pipelines - so the VERSION-COHERENCE half of the in-proc binding
       invariant is satisfied.

       The NAME half is not, and the old comment here claimed otherwise. All four are unsigned, so
       any other extension's pkgdef can CAPTURE one of these names for the whole devenv process -
       exactly what crashed a user's IDE via Markdig (see the Markdig.Signed comment above). This
       island cannot be rescued the same way: there is no signed variant of any of them on nuget.org
       (checked), and it cannot be merged either, because Emoji.Wpf resolves its own resources through
       pack URIs naming itself AND its version -
         /Emoji.Wpf;V0.3.4.0;component/picker.xaml (also textbox, internal/win10flags, win11flags)
       - baked into compiled BAML, which a merge cannot rewrite; and the island is all-or-nothing
       since Emoji.Wpf references Typography and Stfu by name. Note those URIs are UTF-16 in metadata,
       so a UTF-8 scan for "pack://" reports a clean bill of health on an assembly full of them.

       So the residual risk here is real and IRREDUCIBLE by packaging, and is covered by
       MarkdownRenderFirewall instead: a capture degrades to plain text with a notice naming the
       contested assembly rather than killing devenv. Typography.OpenFont 1.0.0.0 is the one to watch
       - a generic name at a generic version. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Emoji.Wpf" Version="0.3.4" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    THERE IS DELIBERATELY NO ILRepack HERE ANY MORE, and re-adding it would be a regression.

    An earlier fix for #103 merged the unsigned Markdig into this assembly with /internalize. It
    worked, but it cost: a merge target, a System.Memory pin to undo the merge's effect on NuGet
    resolution, and a Windows PowerShell 5.1 verification script wired into two release gates, because
    the merged assembly was net472 and nothing else we run offline is. Switching to the strong-named
    Markdig.Signed above buys the same immunity for a package reference, so all of that was removed.

    If a future dependency needs the same treatment, know what the package does before reaching for
    it: ILRepack.Lib.MSBuild.Task ships its OWN default target, AfterTargets="Build", gated only on
    $(Configuration.Contains('Release')) and !Exists('<project dir>\ILRepack.targets'). It merges
    EVERY DLL in the output folder into the primary assembly and then deletes the copy-local
    references. So an ILRepack.targets file existing is what suppresses it, and deleting that file
    does not fall back to "no merging" - it falls back to merging everything. Debug builds are exempt,
    so every check that runs in Debug misses it.

    Prefer, in order: a strong-named package (Markdig.Signed); then merging; and note that some things
    can take neither - Emoji.Wpf resolves its own resources through pack URIs naming itself AND its
    version, baked into compiled BAML that ILRepack cannot rewrite, and has no signed variant on
    nuget.org. That island is covered by MarkdownRenderFirewall instead, permanently.
  -->

</Project>
